//
// A VECTOR local rebound from a fn-ref call frees the store it displaces, at the dense and the
// nullable spelling, and through a forwarding frame (loft#1329, `formal/ownership.md`
// @FR-O-NoDiverge).
//
// loft#1328 closed the same question for a nullable reference, a dense one and a keyed
// collection. The vector destination is its sibling and reached the ceiling three separate
// ways, each a different reader of one fact:
//
//   1. `--native`'s displaced-free gate listed `Reference | Enum | keyed` and not `Vector`,
//      where the interpreter's twin (`state/codegen.rs`'s `owned_ref`) has carried the bare
//      `Vector` arm all along.  One kind, present on one side and absent on the other.
//   2. BOTH backends kept the dep — so they agreed, and the shared fact was short.  A
//      CAPTURING lambda's assignment is a block that mints the closure record, WRITES each
//      capture into it, and then yields the `FnRef`; a capture that is itself a fn-ref is
//      written as an `FnRefDnr` argument of that write, so `fnref_target_in`'s tree walk saw
//      two definition numbers and reported the variable as naming TWO targets.  That answer
//      is reserved for a slot two lambdas were assigned to, and it made the callee
//      unresolvable, which is what declined the free.  A capture is a payload, not a
//      candidate.
//   3. The NULLABLE spelling could not be measured at all: a lambda declaring
//      `-> vector<τ>?` aborted the compiler on the H5 two-pass contract, because the
//      between-passes buffer sweep asked "does this deliver a collection?" through
//      `ret_promo_base` (which peels `Optional(Vector)`) and then rejected the same
//      definition on the RAW type — so no buffer was reserved and pass 2 GREW the attribute.
//      With that closed, the nullable destination grew the peak exactly like the dense one.
//
// ⚠ THE EXIT-LEAK GATE CANNOT SEE THIS CLASS, AND THAT IS WHY THE CELLS COUNT TO 70 000.
// The frame frees everything, so at 200 iterations every cell here reports no leak on both
// backends and answers correctly; what grows is the PEAK, 1:1 with the iteration count. At
// 70 000 — past the 65 535-store table — the run aborts with `store table exhausted`. The
// ceiling turns a watermark into an accept/reject split, which is the channel a guard can
// score. `LOFT_STORES=timeline`'s `peak` is the instrument that measures it cheaply; a leak
// cell reads green on every broken build.
//
// ⚠ AND THE CONTROLS ARE NOT FILLER.  The direct-call cell was correct throughout, which is
// what says the missing thing was the fn-ref spelling rather than the free.  The
// capture-returning and destination-reading cells are the over-free direction: each widening
// here licenses a free, and a free taken on a store the caller still owns answers wrong or
// faults instead of aborting.
//
// @falsified-at: a8c0b74d — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.
//   The whole-file verdict is a floor, and here it hides most of what moved: the file's
//   FIRST failure on that build is a PARSE abort, so `make falsify` never reaches the
//   ceiling cells at all. Per-cell census, each run standalone against the control build —
//   10 of the 16 (cell x backend) runs fail there, and all three controls pass:
//
//     cell                                   interpret               native
//     dense vector rebound                   pass                    store table exhausted
//     nullable vector rebound                ICE (H5 two-pass)       ICE (H5 two-pass)
//     forwarding fn-ref                      store table exhausted   store table exhausted
//     nullable collection, null arm          ICE (H5 two-pass)       ICE (H5 two-pass)
//     callee reads the destination           pass                    store table exhausted
//     direct call                    CONTROL pass                    pass
//     closure hands back its capture CONTROL pass                    pass
//     values at every step           CONTROL pass                    pass
//
//   Two channels carry it and neither is the assert channel: PANIC for the ceiling cells,
//   which die inside the loop before any assertion is reached, and the PARSER for the
//   nullable ones, which never produce a program at all. The value asserts beside each
//   ceiling are what would catch a displaced free that released the WRONG store — a fault
//   no ceiling can see, and the reason the three controls are cells rather than a comment.
//   The dense cell passing on the INTERPRETER is the same fact as its native failure:
//   `owned_ref` has carried the bare `Vector` arm all along, and only this side was short.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

fn mk1329(i: integer) -> vector<integer> { [i, i + 1] }

fn test_a_dense_vector_rebound_from_a_fn_ref() {
  // The filed shape. `--native` held one store per iteration where the interpreter was flat.
  m = fn(i: integer) -> vector<integer> { [i, i + 1] };
  x: vector<integer> = [];
  for i in 0..70000 { x = m(i); }
  assert(len(x) == 2, "the length: got {len(x)}");
  assert(x[0] == 69999, "the last value: got {x[0]}");
}

fn test_a_nullable_vector_rebound_from_a_fn_ref() {
  // The `?` belongs to the value the caller reads, not to the storage — so the nullable
  // destination owns its store exactly as the dense one does. Unmeasurable until the lambda
  // stopped aborting the compiler, and then broken on BOTH backends.
  m = fn(i: integer) -> vector<integer>? { [i, i + 1] };
  x: vector<integer>? = null;
  for i in 0..70000 { x = m(i); }
  assert(len(x ?? []) == 2, "the length: got {len(x ?? [])}");
  assert((x ?? [0])[0] == 69999, "the last value: got {(x ?? [0])[0]}");
}

fn test_a_fn_ref_that_forwards_to_another_fn_ref() {
  // The forwarding frame, and the cell that fails on BOTH backends: `fwd` CAPTURES `inner`,
  // so its closure record is built with an `FnRefDnr` write that the target walk counted as
  // a second candidate. The inner call's buffer then travelled up to this frame and was
  // released only at its exit — one store per iteration.
  inner = fn(i: integer) -> vector<integer> { [i, i + 1] };
  fwd = fn(i: integer) -> vector<integer> { inner(i) };
  x: vector<integer> = [];
  for i in 0..70000 { x = fwd(i); }
  assert(len(x) == 2, "the length: got {len(x)}");
  assert(x[0] == 69999, "the last value: got {x[0]}");
}

fn test_the_direct_call_spelling_is_unmoved() {
  // Correct throughout — the control that says the missing thing was the SPELLING, not the
  // free. A direct call is handed a caller-local `__ref_N` buffer and reuses it every
  // iteration.
  x: vector<integer> = [];
  for i in 0..70000 { x = mk1329(i); }
  assert(x[0] == 69999, "the last value: got {x[0]}");
}

fn test_a_closure_that_hands_back_its_capture() {
  // The over-free direction. The store that comes back is the CAPTURE's, minted before the
  // call and belonging to the enclosing scope — so the rebind must not release it. A free
  // here answers wrong or faults rather than aborting, which is why the base is read after
  // the loop as well as through `x`.
  base: vector<integer> = [7, 8];
  m = fn(i: integer) -> vector<integer> { base };
  x: vector<integer> = [];
  for i in 0..70000 { x = m(i); }
  assert(x[0] == 7, "the value through x: got {x[0]}");
  assert(base[0] == 7, "the capture is intact: got {base[0]}");
  assert(len(base) == 2, "the capture's length: got {len(base)}");
}

fn test_a_callee_that_reads_the_destination() {
  // The other over-free direction, and the reason the free is `_old != place` AFTER the call
  // rather than a free before it: the callee READS the store being displaced. Freeing early
  // would hand it a released record, and freeing the SAME store would release a live one.
  m = fn(v: vector<integer>) -> vector<integer> { [len(v) + 1] };
  x: vector<integer> = [0];
  for _ in 0..70000 { x = m(x); }
  assert(len(x) == 1, "the length: got {len(x)}");
  assert(x[0] == 2, "the accumulated value: got {x[0]}");
}

fn test_a_nullable_collection_lambda_with_a_null_arm() {
  // Reserving the buffer for a nullable collection return is only half of it: the native
  // fn-ref dispatch decides whether to MINT that buffer, and it read the raw return type
  // too — so this candidate was handed `DbRef::NULL` and its delivery wrote through it.
  // `vector_add` carries no `.rec != 0` test, so the present arm faulted where the absent
  // arm was quietly fine. Both arms are exercised.
  m = fn(i: integer) -> vector<integer>? { if i > 0 { [i] } else { null } };
  present = m(3);
  absent = m(0);
  assert(len(present ?? []) == 1, "the present arm's length: got {len(present ?? [])}");
  assert((present ?? [0])[0] == 3, "the present arm's value: got {(present ?? [0])[0]}");
  assert(len(absent ?? []) == 0, "the absent arm is empty: got {len(absent ?? [])}");
}

fn test_the_values_are_right_at_every_step() {
  // An abort is not the only way this can be wrong. A displaced free that released the WRONG
  // store answers here instead of crashing, and no ceiling cell can see that — so every
  // iteration's value is folded into one number.
  m = fn(i: integer) -> vector<integer> { [i, i + 1] };
  x: vector<integer> = [];
  s = 0;
  for i in 0..5 { x = m(i); s += x[0] * 10 + x[1]; }
  assert(s == 115, "the per-step values: got {s}");
}
